\f0\lang2057 P:\line Write a function that accepts a string as an argument and performs operations based on the commands in that string, as outlined above.\line\line input: string\line output: number/s\line\line rules: the `stack` has an initial value of [], and `register` has an initial value of 0\line\line What is the difference between `add` and `pop`?  - is it `add` when there's already a value in the register? \line\line\line E: \line see above\line\line D: \line arrays, strings \line\line A:\line we need to first parse the string by splitting it into its separate words, then processing the commands one by one. Convert numeric strings into numbers? \line\line  - initialise `stack` to `[]`\line - initialise `register` to `0`\line  - initialise `commandArr` to the `argStr`, split into separate words\line  - TRANSFORM `commandArr` such that any numeric strings are converted to numbers\line  - ITERATE through `commandArr`\line  ---  initialise `currCommand` to the current value \line --- IF the `currCommand` is `PRINT`, log the value of `register` to the console\line  -- IF `currCommand` is an integer, add it to `register`\line --- IF `currCommand` is `PUSH`, add `register` to the `stack`\line  --- IF `currCommand` is `ADD`, remove the last value from `stack` and add it to `register`\line  --- IF `currCommand` is `SUB`, remove the last value from `stack` and minus it from `register`\line ---IF `currCommand` is `MULT`, remove the last value from `stack`and multiply it by `register`,\line -----storing the result in `register`\line --- IF `currCommand` is `DIV` remove the last value from `stack` and divide `register` by this \line ---- value, storing the integer result in `register`\line  --- IF `currCommand` is `REMAINDER`, remove the last value from `stack` and divide the `register` by the removed (popped) value, storing the integer remainder in the `register`\line ---- IF `currCommand` is `POP`, remove the last item from `stack` and put it in `register`\line  \line\line\par
